#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221157 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "11b5421d-e98b-5e37-9bdd-bec13f010118" :question/math-academy-id "e-21503" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\}  = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$  find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t) = \\cos(\\pi t).$ Thus, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "58ed1920-cd4f-5993-b3bf-1fa4e43492af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21527" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221163 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "602b037e-28bf-5afd-a466-652668bf08c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21551" :question/problem "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddb6627-9aab-5952-b2ce-159bb3d012c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300184} {:answer/id #uuid "2fadd4e7-e733-5dd7-9e6e-56bb35b97cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300193} {:answer/id #uuid "4befee37-8f81-5b4e-a2a7-cfb1aae0d977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300357} {:answer/id #uuid "7acb2c8f-f244-5570-a50e-e921d09fa87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300570} {:answer/id #uuid "87393951-93a6-5680-8792-91d8b0b433e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acb2c8f-f244-5570-a50e-e921d09fa87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300570} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f06649-29ac-5ed0-8693-7d88b83ad795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "323eedee-f02a-50b8-b6e9-9655eac05ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327935" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{L}^{-1}{\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f7e5166-76a9-5f1f-81f2-76e949ad5931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220226} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ec395-dca6-52a1-b395-c96cf7010238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220227} {:answer/id #uuid "a02e26e4-d905-5d54-9e91-32505bcd6f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220228} {:answer/id #uuid "bf96dad9-de53-5d97-a8e4-b50e65835544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220229} {:answer/id #uuid "e65286a5-f85f-5377-bace-9462e1d66fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220230}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3e1d20-c247-5e43-896a-e0df80d1c646" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220225}] :question/id #uuid "e1980651-68dd-5422-8d9a-998a7b723dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-330822" :question/problem "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$ for $s > 0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}τ^{2}dτ$\nfor $s > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06802c47-a352-526d-aa42-6ea70b69cd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302499} {:answer/id #uuid "31006540-4f0e-543e-bc68-5adb6ecfd2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302764} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6ba575-0c9b-5053-9fbd-17ede9965369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302826} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc36011-bc52-5787-9951-7552a3914c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303431} {:answer/id #uuid "be43cfb0-d748-5bad-a011-749c230baa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be43cfb0-d748-5bad-a011-749c230baa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303621} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d4daa5-138e-5de2-a176-e7cd9c9e605f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "104a4fc1-fc3a-583e-ac36-a24bfc2670cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-330831" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{3}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012cec05-92d1-5798-a767-621527d58c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302456} {:answer/id #uuid "3857e50f-adec-5e95-9bb0-3b493942d1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302804} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad49800-edde-5768-ace9-4dcfe368023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302927} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb496d2-2774-5dab-abdc-4cbe2096ec4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303343} {:answer/id #uuid "c332c53f-6fbe-5b2e-9025-eb87ebb84156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad49800-edde-5768-ace9-4dcfe368023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302927} :answer-field/id #uuid "4618ac30-5dc6-5b42-a655-bca47677562b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "510ae8c4-1f31-5a18-9a74-826f9d3a4047" :question/math-academy-id "q-330835" :question/problem "Given that $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a3c959-75ca-5441-bcf3-23d4f787222c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302479} {:answer/id #uuid "1bef64cc-0d2c-5f98-a507-02d1052185e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302638} {:answer/id #uuid "71add469-2d90-55dc-be03-2e3a39950b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303145} {:answer/id #uuid "8487079f-256c-548b-9b50-71e2a3f7c818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303282} {:answer/id #uuid "b14358a0-e002-52d4-aaa1-35404f1569bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71add469-2d90-55dc-be03-2e3a39950b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303145} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d309ae-5289-56fe-8e78-f5864b8353f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "409ed1af-a518-5652-a26f-cb341eaa1c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-330838" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae71c8ab-fe3a-550d-bdaa-4be4ea854604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220238} {:answer/id #uuid "23621895-c52c-5c66-881d-d43268431a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220239} {:answer/id #uuid "324d7c73-5a39-5b07-8808-675a6cd26d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220240} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcb6c4f-9342-5631-9a9d-4a86c60412ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220241} {:answer/id #uuid "58cfb452-ca17-5564-ad0d-b139f307149e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{4}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220242}] :answer-field/id #uuid "01c6a3d3-7631-583a-b8b1-21c64913429c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220237}] :question/id #uuid "ffb65743-685e-5524-b4b3-34401a7e30c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-330840" :question/problem "For the function\n$G(s) = \\frac{2}{s^{4}}, s > 0$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f80b316-6731-5f5a-9dec-739fd3be6c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{720}{{t}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302559} {:answer/id #uuid "25d0f0bf-2b54-519f-94e7-adf5da66e6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302700} {:answer/id #uuid "42d5d705-b17e-599c-8fd8-42db38dbf6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{8}}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302880} {:answer/id #uuid "902b0ead-51de-5d7b-bd8f-d5add3c46114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303356} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cdff7d-b0fc-5867-b70b-88bd55c14f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6cdff7d-b0fc-5867-b70b-88bd55c14f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303589} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e69e93c-a037-567a-be3b-ba40f212fcb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37553079-b450-5c8e-9348-e4ed61dd6d77" :question/math-academy-id "q-330845" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{6!}{{s}^{6+1}}} \\\\  & ={t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{6}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
